<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick the Fair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1289–<a href="/years/1330">1330</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Frederick I of Austria · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick the Fair was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> from 1308 and German anti-king from 1314, elected by one faction against Louis IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Defeated and captured by Louis at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhldorf">Battle of Mühldorf</a> in 1322, he was held prisoner until 1325, when the two rivals agreed to share the royal title. He had little real power thereafter and died in 1330.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-the-fair.jpg" alt="Frederick the Fair" width="320" height="329" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karl von Sava · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slicny2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the second son of King Albert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Elisabeth of Gorizia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, and a grandson of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> king of Germany, Rudolph I. In 1298 his father invested him and his elder brother Rudolph III with the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>. After Rudolph's early death in 1307 and the assassination of Albert in 1308, Frederick ruled both duchies for himself and his younger brothers. The royal title passed to Count Henry VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, and Frederick had to abjure all claims to the German crown, receiving in return the confirmation of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Frederick had been a friend of his cousin Louis IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, who also grew up at the Austrian court. They came to arms in 1313, when Bavarian nobles entrusted Frederick with the guardianship of the sons of the late Duke Stephen I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a>. Louis beat him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gammelsdorf">Battle of Gammelsdorf</a> on 9 November 1313. After Henry VII died, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> met at Frankfurt-Sachsenhausen on 19 October 1314 and gave Frederick four of seven votes, two of which were contested. The next day a second election chose Louis with five votes. Louis was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and Frederick at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> on 25 November 1314.</p><p>Frederick suffered a crushing defeat at Morgarten in 1315 against the Swiss Confederacy. After years of war, with the support of his brother Leopold I, his army was beaten near Mühldorf on 28 September 1322, and he and 1,300 nobles were captured. Louis held him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trausnitz-castle">Trausnitz Castle</a> for three years. In the Treaty of Trausnitz of 13 March 1325 Frederick recognised Louis as the legitimate ruler and promised to return to captivity if he could not persuade his brothers to submit. Leopold refused, and Frederick went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> as a prisoner, even though the Pope had released him from his oath.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Louis, impressed, renewed their friendship, and the two agreed to rule jointly, which the Pope and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> opposed. A treaty at Ulm on 7 January 1326 had Frederick govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> as king while Louis was to be crowned emperor. After Leopold died in 1326 Frederick withdrew to rule only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>. He died on 13 January 1330 at Gutenstein Castle and was buried at Mauerbach Charterhouse, and later in St. Stephen's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. His return to captivity inspired Schiller's poem Deutsche Treue.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick the Fair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke Louis IV, elected king of the Romans, defeated his Habsburg rival <b>Frederick the Fair</b> at the Battle of Mühldorf in 1322.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Louis of Upper Bavaria defeated his cousin <b>Frederick the Fair</b>, the Habsburg duke of Austria, in a dispute over the guardianship of the young dukes of Lower Bavaria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-9">9 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Mühldorf am Inn, Louis IV of Bavaria defeated and captured his cousin and rival king <b>Frederick the Fair</b> of Austria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-28">28 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis IV was elected King of the Romans in 1314, defeated <b>Frederick the Fair</b> at Mühldorf in 1322 and was crowned emperor in Rome in 1328.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-upper-bavaria">Duchy of Upper Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Gutenstein, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1315" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick the Fair"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1315">What was here in 1315? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
